fix(memory): carry the copy a cancellation left, and hand Compose the path that was checked

Round 20 re-review, GPT-5.6 Terra. Four accepted findings.

A request that ends while the bytes are off their name leaves them
exactly where a failed unlink does, and the cancellation said nothing
about it -- so the caller holding the record read "the request ended",
wrote nothing down, and the tidying later released the row for a path
that holds nothing. The cancellation carries the marker now, on both
roads, and so does a promoted original kept under a name of the vault's
own making.

The sweep's clean answer is a durable one: a missing inbox is refused
when the vault itself is not there, and the directory is flushed before
"there is no second copy" is reported, because an entry that went away
between the listing and the look went away in a page cache.

Compose takes its own options before the subcommand, so
`--project-name x down` was being read as a launch and run through the
launch checks -- refusing a teardown over the thing it was being run to
fix. The verb is now found past those options.

And Compose reads the shell environment ahead of --env-file, so a value
exported in a terminal beat the one this script had just validated. The
launch hands Compose the checked value explicitly.

Two more are answered rather than changed and are written down in the
governance document: a copy of a proposal too large to read, and a copy
of bytes the user rewrote after the decision read them, cannot be
identified by hash and so are named in the failure rather than swept.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
